#include "Generated.xcconfig"

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Bundle identity — the CONFIGURATION's default, not the artifact's identity
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// PRODUCT_BUNDLE_IDENTIFIER is `com.kararfinance.app$(KARAR_BUNDLE_ID_SUFFIX)`.
// The suffix is empty HERE, which makes the seed the production identifier —
// correct for this file, because Release and Profile are the configurations a
// production artifact is cut from and PRODUCT_BUNDLE_IDENTIFIER is what a
// provisioning profile is ever selected by. It is not, on its own, what the
// artifact ends up called.
//
// THE ASYMMETRY WITH ANDROID THAT USED TO LIVE HERE IS CLOSED.
//
// Android derives its suffix from `-Pkarar.env` and cross-checks it against the
// compiled `--dart-define=KARAR_ENV`, so a DEV package cannot claim production
// identity. iOS could not do the same in an xcconfig, and for a real reason:
// PRODUCT_BUNDLE_IDENTIFIER is resolved by Xcode from build settings before any
// script runs, and while the Flutter tool DOES forward the dart-defines into
// the Xcode build, it forwards them as DART_DEFINES — one comma-separated list
// of base64-encoded KEY=VALUE entries — which xcconfig has no string function
// capable of decoding. The conclusion drawn from that was that the suffix had
// to wait for per-environment Xcode configurations and schemes; the consequence
// was that LOCAL, DEV, STAGING and PRODUCTION all produced
// `com.kararfinance.app`.
//
// What actually follows from it is narrower: the rule cannot live in an
// xcconfig, so it lives in Scripts/bundle_identity.sh, and the `Verify Packaged
// Bundle` build phase — which can decode DART_DEFINES, and already does, to
// decide the App Transport Security exception — applies it to the packaged
// plist and fails the build when the compiled environment and the packaged
// identity cannot be made to agree. A LOCAL, DEV or STAGING artifact now
// carries `.local`, `.dev` or `.staging`, and a PRODUCTION artifact is the only
// unsuffixed one.
//
// Per-environment Xcode configurations remain the right long-term mechanism and
// remain BLOCKED: they need per-environment App Store records and a real Apple
// Team ID, and this repository holds none and invents none. The build phase
// refuses to narrow an identifier on any build that has a DEVELOPMENT_TEAM or a
// PROVISIONING_PROFILE_SPECIFIER, so the day that work is real, the shortcut
// fails loudly rather than shipping an artifact whose identity its own profile
// does not cover. No App Store record exists for any of these identifiers and
// this phase creates none, so nothing is reserved and nothing is squatted.
//
// The canonical per-environment build commands are in Scripts/bundle_identity.sh
// beside the rule they produce.

KARAR_BUNDLE_ID_SUFFIX =

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// App Transport Security
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// A Release or Profile artifact carries no NSAppTransportSecurity key at all
// and gets the unmodified platform transport policy: TLS validated normally,
// no exception domain, no arbitrary-load key of any kind.
//
// That is not achieved by a setting in this file. Runner/Info.plist has no
// such key in any configuration, and the local-development exception in
// Runner/ATSLocalDevelopment.plist is merged into the packaged plist only by
// the `Verify Packaged Bundle` build phase, only for a Debug build whose
// compiled KARAR_ENV is LOCAL. There is deliberately nothing here to switch
// off, because a switch is a thing that can be left in the wrong position.

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Release signing
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// No signing identity, team identifier or provisioning profile is set here,
// and no certificate or profile is committed anywhere in this repository.
// Release signing material is supplied EXTERNALLY at release time by the
// release operator or a release pipeline, and is never checked in.
//
// Every artifact this repository's CI produces is a simulator or debug build,
// which requires no signing material at all. No CI job holds an Apple
// credential, and none needs one.
